feat(core): hardware probe, split planner, cluster manager, QR pairing

The node can now divide a model across devices and actually run it.

New modules:
- hardware.rs  probes RAM/cores/threads and derives a memory budget, so a
               device never promises memory it cannot spare. Phone numbers
               arrive over POST /peers instead of being assumed.
- plan.rs      split planner: distributes layers by budget share, charges KV
               cache per layer, reports shortfall and a plain-English summary.
- cluster.rs   runs the real thing: spawns ggml-rpc-server, then llama-server
               with --rpc <self>,<peers>, waits for /health, supervises exits.
               rpc_reachable() gates whether a peer counts at all.
- qr.rs        pairing payload + terminal rendering. Encoder delegates to the
               qrcode crate: a hand-written one produced grids that looked
               right and did not scan.

New endpoints: POST/GET /peers, GET/POST /models, GET /cluster,
POST /cluster/plan, POST /cluster/start, POST /cluster/stop.
/pair now returns a privateml:// URI plus this device's hardware.

Honesty fix: a peer that reports hardware but has no reachable RPC worker is
excluded from plans and from the pool total, with the reason surfaced. An
earlier demo had counted a fabricated phone and it silently inflated memory.

verified end-to-end on real binaries:
- probe: 7720 MB RAM -> 6320 MB budget, 3 threads
- real rpc worker on :50111 counted, fake peer on :50052 excluded
- plan: "Fits across 2 devices: local-rpc 19/36L + anon 17/36L"
- start -> state=running -> chat returned 'SPLIT WORKS' through the split
- QR payload decodes exactly with OpenCV (version 4, 37x37 modules)

tests: 27 passing
